#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124557 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where All Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "094c985d-13f5-5f69-aeaa-14eacd51632b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409539} {:answer/id #uuid "1b8221d1-3113-502e-8743-5e1721ad92fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409540} {:answer/id #uuid "65397964-92f2-5396-b4dd-4062f6c76e9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0,\\,y\\ne 0,\\,z\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409545} {:answer/id #uuid "83b42671-0c9a-50d8-9e14-5be3dd21c197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz\\ge 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409547} {:answer/id #uuid "fe8bb828-cf39-59be-bb0e-11477fd6ff9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0,\\,x+y+z\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "094c985d-13f5-5f69-aeaa-14eacd51632b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409539} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3c16b06-37a6-5eac-b3ba-318617c8302e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5744243e-c58b-5537-916f-bee0cb473253" :question/math-academy-id "q-132654" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$ is defined only when $xyz>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z}$ is defined only when $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268907 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25ba8136-3f97-5995-83e3-95432551c0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266481} {:answer/id #uuid "a1cedf13-a8c5-5f74-9ead-309286311701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267599} {:answer/id #uuid "a2810b5e-9362-516a-94d2-1ba39e99e36b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267605} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4bcb4b-ab4b-5be4-b1d4-d1183b7511c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267681} {:answer/id #uuid "cfeb3149-070f-5012-8119-2a98c9b1323e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1cedf13-a8c5-5f74-9ead-309286311701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267599} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb59d576-4495-5a01-a6f2-41656c1c4ed5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9dc50a8a-45d6-510c-8209-df3404b44621" :question/math-academy-id "q-132680" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}?$\n\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(4,2)$\n- ${f}_{x}(0,2)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{y}(0,2)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(4,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(4,2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{{2}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(4,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(4,2) & =\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})\\sqrt{4}}=\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {f}_{y}(4,2) & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{4}}{{2}^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,2)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,2)=\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{0}}{{2}^{2}}=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(0,2)=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{0}}{{2}^{3}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "313ed8a6-5624-51e9-8247-921676b60bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266577} {:answer/id #uuid "409a59c0-29a1-5253-ba63-e612221ceedc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266729} {:answer/id #uuid "635f6241-0e3a-5ddf-84d9-85447f3da39e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267018} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e2402e-0272-58c9-af19-86fc33ef5471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268033} {:answer/id #uuid "e54c682f-12fe-57b4-a4b2-763baf12853b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "409a59c0-29a1-5253-ba63-e612221ceedc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266729} :answer-field/id #uuid "61fa50ca-fd4a-5d40-a9dd-ff3a34de4409" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a68d3690-fbcf-53d1-acbf-a5134efed5cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-132728" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2)?$\n\nI. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(0,2)$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(1,0)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{y}(1,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(2)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is also false. If we substitute $(1,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{1}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124557 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where All Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f815155-04df-505a-b90c-02233f925ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382209} {:answer/id #uuid "a251a802-17c8-5538-b07d-0284f94244c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382210} {:answer/id #uuid "a45af148-f324-5595-94fb-d8a75c7d31ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0,z\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382211} {:answer/id #uuid "b4615464-f44d-598c-81f7-059882147bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ne 0,z<0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382212} {:answer/id #uuid "c532f18e-2b4d-55ec-8919-7a91f96a1f01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy=0,z\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a251a802-17c8-5538-b07d-0284f94244c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382210} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb805034-5a71-5881-8d6e-dc2faac4f6f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07b7a4d5-0d88-5759-b677-08d7e8ac3fca" :question/math-academy-id "q-72007" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{z}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{y{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2z\\sqrt{xy}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2z\\sqrt{xy}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{z}$ is defined only when $xy\\ge 0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}=\\frac{y{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2z\\sqrt{xy}}$ is defined only when $xy>0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${f}_{y}=\\frac{x{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2z\\sqrt{xy}}$ is the same\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{z}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{{z}^{2}}$ is defined only when $xy\\ge 0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0,z\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc40fcb0-2c2a-55a5-928c-2e67c7706d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382223} {:answer/id #uuid "803704d4-c54d-51d4-bb8b-9ba89d1a68a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382224} {:answer/id #uuid "351491f0-64f5-5dbf-9e28-539ac7d3016b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382225} {:answer/id #uuid "d3eba417-0d7f-5f23-9c6b-5c408b631ccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382226} {:answer/id #uuid "3437567a-224b-5bc5-a548-d70ef0e0e103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "351491f0-64f5-5dbf-9e28-539ac7d3016b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382225} :answer-field/id #uuid "c50da896-45b8-57cb-91f7-c47a8450d191" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff98942c-b72f-558c-86fd-786f6575a16d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72019" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z}?$ \n\n- ${f}_{y}(1,1,1)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{z}(0,1,2)$ is defined\n- The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(0,1,1)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+y-z}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1,1)=\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+1-1}=\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,1,1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+1-1}}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,1,2)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1,2)=\\sqrt{2\\cdot 0+1-2}=\\sqrt{-1},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since the number under the radical is negative.\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(0,1,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1,1)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 0+1-1}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only'."}]}
